    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Calcineurin (CN) is a highly-conserved serine/threonine phosphatase that is projected to interact with at least 300 proteins in the human proteome, only 27 of which have been experimentally confirmed to date. These proteins are known to interact with CN via at least one of two well-characterized amino acid binding motifs: the PxIxIT and the LxVP. C16orf74 is an uncharacterized protein that contains a candidate PxIxIT motif and which has been identified as a potential CN substrate by several independent screening techniques, including yeast two-hybrid, phage display, and computational methods. While previous work has established a correlation between C16orf74 expression and various cancers, little has been done to characterize this protein’s interaction with CN or to explore the implications of that interaction. To isolate the most essential residues in the C16orf74 PxIxIT motif, we created several versions of C16orf74 with strategically placed alanine mutations. We used these C16orf74 mutants to conduct in vitro and in vivo binding assays to quantify the strength of the CN-C16orf74 interaction. A luciferase reporter assay that uses NFAT-activated transcription as a proxy for CN activity was used to measure the effect of C16orf74 expression on CN function in HEK293 cells. This data combined with previously published information about C16orf74 localization from immunofluorescence microscopy suggests that C16orf74 is an inhibitor of CN that acts through the PxIxIT binding motif at the plasma membrane. This discovery expands our knowledge of CN signaling networks in humans, a network which is already known to be critical for T-cell activation, heart-valve development, and some aspects of learning and memory.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
